{"records":[{"id":"4dyfj6mdw3","title":"Expression matrix of transcriptomics","description":"Transcriptomics data are intended to prompt research on the relevant mechanism and to use that mechanism as a basis for further experiments. Through the transcriptomics of the TLR2/MAPK/NF-κB pathway, how Ustiloxins leads to kidney injury through the TLR2/MAPK/NF-κB pathway is explained step by step.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-23T21:37:31.132Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4dyfj6mdw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4dyfj6mdw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yingchun","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j8p3phfsyb","title":"Xanthium Alleviates LPS-induced  Inflammation  by Inhibition of JAK/STAT, NF-κB, and MAPK Pathways in Macrophages","description":"Xanthium (Xa) is one of the active ingredients of Xanthium strumarium L. . Here, we focused on exploring the inhibitory effect of Xanthium on inflammation and the potential anti-inflammatory pathways. After LPS-stimulation of the cells, the generation of NO and associated pro-inflammatory factors (TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6) increased, whereas both were reduced after pretreatment with Xanthium. However, IL-10, an anti-inflammatory factor, was increased by LPS stimulation, which was reduced by Xanthium pretreatment. Furthermore, when compared to LPS-stimulated cells alone, the mRNA expression of nitric oxide synthase (iNOS) and cyclooxygenase-2 (COX2), as well as TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6, was considerably downregulated and suppressed after pretreatment with Xanthium. In addition, Xanthium showed inhibitory effects on iNOS and COX2 at the protein level. Xanthium inhibits both the STAT3 and NF-κB signaling pathways. Xanthium suppresses the inflammatory reaction generated by LPS and exerts anti-inflammatory effects mainly through JAK/STAT, NF-κB and MAPK pathways, among others. Therefore, Xanthium could be a good anti-inflammatory medication candidate.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-21T12:31:59.203Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j8p3phfsyb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j8p3phfsyb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuanqi","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenyu","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanhui","last_name":"Kong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fang","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huanan","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kun","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nf3hm5dj9m","title":"ALI data","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:14.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nf3hm5dj9m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nf3hm5dj9m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jingtao","last_name":"LI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Respiratory System"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ss6ghkd8bk","title":"Inhibition of M1 polarization in alveolar macrophages by vasoactive intestinal peptide attenuates LPS-induced acute lung injury in vivo and vitro","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:35.873Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ss6ghkd8bk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ss6ghkd8bk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"honggang","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Injury"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3sknzkyfns","title":"Distinct roles of canonical and non-canonical NF-κB signaling in protective immunity during Plasmodium yoelii infection","description":"Folder including data for Distinct roles of canonical and non-canonical NF-κB signaling in protective immunity during Plasmodium yoelii infection","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-30T04:15:40.072Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3sknzkyfns","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3sknzkyfns"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yaming","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"246p9b4tp4","title":"SMZJ Alleviate Tourette Syndrome-Research Data","description":"Research Data  of the enclosed manuscript entitled “Shao-Ma-Zhi-Jing Granules Alleviate Tourette Syndrome by Modulating the cAMP/PI3K/AKT/NF-κB Signaling Pathway, T Cell Differentiation, Microglia, and Gut Microbiota”.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-24T10:29:34.367Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/246p9b4tp4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/246p9b4tp4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Linying","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jfvd5k43mw","title":"Xanthatin alleviates LPS-induced inflammatory response in RAW2647 macrophages by inhibiting toll-like receptor 4 pathway","description":"Xanthatin (XT) is a sesquiterpene lactone isolated from the Chinese herb Xanthium, which belongs to the Asteraceae family. Here, we developed an in-flammation model via stimulating RAW 264.7 cells with LPS, which was applied to assess the anti-inflammatory effect and probable mechanisms of xanthatin. Pretreatment with of xanthatin lowered the amount of NO and associated pro-inflammatory factors (TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6), which were enhanced in the cells after LPS-activated. Interestingly, RAW 264.7 cells were pre-treated with xanthatin, the mRNA expression of iNOS, COX-2, TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6 was downregulated and repressed as compared with the only LPS-induced group. Furthermore, phosphorylated levels of related proteins (STAT3, ERK1/2, SAPK/JNK, IκBα, p65) were notably increased with the only LPS-activated cells. Interestingly, these phosphorylated proteins could be reverted by pre-treating with xanthatin in a dose-dependent way. Additionally, xanthatin was involved to block NF-κB p65 from translocating into the nucleus and activating inflamma-tory gene transcription. Collectively, these results demonstrated that xanthatin suppresseeds the inflammatory effect caused by LPS through downregulating NF-κB, MAPK and STATs signaling pathways. Taken together, xanthatin pos-sesses the potential to act as a good anti-inflammatory medication candidate.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-20T22:02:36.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jfvd5k43mw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jfvd5k43mw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuanqi","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ryvrgm4k8z","title":"Synchronization of transcription steps defines the major bottlenecks in HIV-1 latency reactivation","description":"Western blot images for manuscript entitled \"Synchronization of transcription steps defines the major bottlenecks in HIV-1 latency reactivation\" by Hyder, Challa et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-28T08:32:25.71Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ryvrgm4k8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ryvrgm4k8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"D'Orso","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Usman","last_name":"Hyder","orcid":"0000-0002-7655-1533"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Gene Regulation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hngpdgb82z","title":"Hydroxytyrosol reduces BPS-induced neuroinflammation by regulating microglia polarization through inhibiting oxidative stress by inactivating of NF-κB signaling pathway","description":"This study mainly studied the toxic effect of BPS on microglia, and induced oxidative stress by activating NF-κB signaling pathway to polarize microglia towards M1, inhibit M2 polarization and promote inflammation, thus causing damage to the central nervous system; while hydroxytyrosol treatment reversed the effects of BPS, polarized microglia towards M2, inhibited inflammation. Protects the central nervous system from neuroinflammation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-24T09:11:18.701Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hngpdgb82z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hngpdgb82z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhang","last_name":"ZH","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun","last_name":"Hou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guoshun","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Toxicology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tk52nhj67c","title":"Inhibition of phosphodiesterase 4 alleviates mastitis induced by Staphylococcus aureus via MAPK and NF-κB signaling pathways","description":"Table S1.qRT-PCR Primer Sequence List","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-27T12:30:37.262Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tk52nhj67c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tk52nhj67c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fuxiang","last_name":"Bao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yueqing","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuxuan","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name"